"Vision - Claude Platform Docs (https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/vision)\nciteturn12view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/vision\",\"lineno\":247}); Total lines: 439\nL184: \nL185: \nL186:     `image1_data = \"iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAIAAACQd1PeAAAADElEQVR4nGP4z8AAAAMBAQDJ/pLvAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC\"\nL187:     image2_data = \"iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAIAAACQd1PeAAAADElEQVR4nGNgYPgPAAEDAQAIicLsAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC\"\nL188: \nL189:     client = anthropic.Anthropic()\nL190:     message = client.messages.create(\nL191:         model=\"claude-opus-5-5\",\nL192:         max_tokens=1024,\nL193:         messages=[\nL194:             {\nL195:                 \"role\": \"user\",\nL196:                 \"content\": [\nL197:                     {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Image 1:\"},\nL198:                     {\nL199:                         \"type\": \"image\",\nL200:                         \"source\": {\nL201:                             \"type\": \"base64\",\nL202:                             \"media_type\": \"image/png\",\nL203:                             \"data\": image1_data,\nL204:                         },\nL205:                     },\nL206:                     {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Image 2:\"},\nL207:                     {\nL208:                         \"type\": \"image\",\nL209:                         \"source\": {\nL210:                             \"type\": \"base64\",\nL211:                             \"media_type\": \"image/png\",\nL212:                             \"data\": image2_data,\nL213:                         },\nL214:                     },\nL215:                     {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"How are these images different?\"},\nL216:                 ],\nL217:             }\nL218:         ],\nL219:     )\nL220:     print(message)`\nL221: In a multi-turn conversation, add new images in later `user` turns the same way. Claude has access to every image from earlier turns, so follow-up questions such as \"Are these similar to the first two?\" work without including the earlier images again in the new turn's content.\nL222: \nL223: * * *\nL224: ## Image limits and costs\nL225: ### Request limits\nL226: \nL227: The maximum number of images for each message or request is:\nL228: \nL229:   * 20 for each message on 【82†claude.ai†claude.ai】\nL230:   * 100 for each request on the API, for models with a 200k-token context window\nL231:   * 600 for each request on the API, for all other models\nL232: \nL233: The maximum dimensions for each image are 8000x8000 px.\nL234: If a single API request contains more than 20 images, a stricter per-image dimension limit applies to every image in that request. All `image` blocks in the request count toward this threshold, including images from earlier conversation turns that you resend and images nested inside `tool_result` content (for example, screenshots returned to the computer use tool). On Amazon Bedrock and Claude Models on Google Cloud (Google managed), document blocks such as PDFs also count toward this threshold.\nL235: Images exceeding the stricter limit are rejected with an `invalid_request_error` whose message references \"many-image requests\" and states the current limit in pixels. To stay under the limit on all platforms, either resize each image so that neither dimension exceeds 2000 px, or keep the request to 20 or fewer image and document blocks.\nL236: The maximum size per image is:\nL237: \nL238:   * 10 MB (base64-encoded) on the Claude API and Claude Models on Google Cloud (Google managed).\nL239:   * 5 MB (base64-encoded) on Amazon Bedrock.\nL240:   * 10 MB on 【82†claude.ai†claude.ai】.\nL241: \nL242: Although the API supports up to 600 images for each request, 【85†request size limits】 can be reached first: 32 MB for standard endpoints, and lower on some partner-operated platforms, such as Amazon Bedrock and Claude Models on Google Cloud (Google managed). For many images, consider 【86†uploading with the Files API】 and referencing by `file_id` to keep request payloads small.\nL243: Even when using the Files API, requests with many large images can fail before reaching the 600-image count. Reduce image dimensions or file sizes (for example, by downsampling) before uploading (see 【87†Resolution and token cost】).\nL244: ### Supported formats\nL245: \nL246: Claude supports JPEG, PNG, GIF, and WebP images (`image/jpeg`, `image/png`, `image/gif`, `image/webp`). Animations are unsupported, and only the first frame is used.\nL247: ### Resolution and token cost\nL248: \nL249: Claude processes images in patches instead of pixels. Each patch is a 28×28-pixel block of the image, referred to as a visual token. An image, therefore, costs `⌈width / 28⌉ × ⌈height / 28⌉` visual tokens.\nL250: Each model has a maximum native image resolution, expressed as a long-edge limit and a visual-token limit. Images larger than either limit are downscaled before processing; see 【88†How Claude resizes and pads images】 for the exact rule.\nL251: The exception is screenshots and zoom images that you return to the 【89†computer use】 and 【90†browser use】 toolsets: the API rejects a `tool_result` image that exceeds the model's limits with a validation error instead of downscaling it, so resize those images in your application before returning them. To have any other oversized image rejected with an error instead of downscaled, set the image block's 【91†`transformations` field】.\nL252: Resolution tier  | Models  | Max long edge  | Max visual tokens\nL253: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL254: High-resolution  | Claude 4.7 and later models  | 2,576 px  | 4,784\nL255: Standard  | All other models  | 1,568 px  | 1,568\nL256: \nL257: High-resolution support is automatic on the listed models and requires no beta header or client-side opt-in.\nL258: \nL259: The following table shows the downsized resolution and visual-token cost for several image sizes on each tier:\nL260: Image size  | Standard tier: downsized to  | Standard tier: tokens  | High-resolution tier: downsized to  | High-resolution tier: tokens\nL261: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL262: 200x200 px (0.04 megapixels)  | Not resized  | 64  | Not resized  | 64\nL263: 1000x1000 px (1 megapixel)  | Not resized  | 1,296  | Not resized  | 1,296\nL264: 1092x1092 px (1.19 megapixels)  | Not resized  | 1,521  | Not resized  | 1,521\nL265: 1920x1080 px (2.07 megapixels)  | 1456x819 px  | 1,560  | Not resized  | 2,691\nL266: 2000x1500 px (3 megapixels)  | 1269x952 px  | 1,564  | Not resized  | 3,888\nL267: 3840x2160 px (8.29 megapixels)  | 1456x819 px  | 1,560  | 2576x1449 px  | 4,784\nL268: When an image is downsized, Claude scales it to the largest size that fits the tier's limits while preserving its aspect ratio. This caps the token cost. For the precise rule and a reference implementation, see 【88†How Claude resizes and pads images】.\nL269: To estimate cost, multiply the token count by the 【92†per-token price of the model†claude.com】 you're using. For example, at Claude Haiku 4.5's $1 USD per million input tokens (standard tier), the 1000×1000 image costs about $1.30 USD per thousand images. At Claude Opus 5.5's $4 USD per million (high-resolution tier), the same image costs about $5.18 USD per thousand and the 4K image about $19.14 USD per thousand.\nL270: High-resolution images can use up to roughly three times more visual tokens than the same image on a standard-tier model. If you don't need the additional fidelity that high resolution provides for computer use, screenshot understanding, and dense documents, downsample images before sending to control token costs. To minimize latency and to simplify 【67†coordinate-based workflows】, prefer resizing images before uploading them.\nL271: ### Image quality guidance\nL272: \nL273: When providing images to Claude, keep the following in mind for best results:\nL274:   * Image clarity: Ensure images are clear and not too blurry or pixelated.\nL275:   * Text: If the image contains important text, make sure it's legible and not too small. Avoid cropping out key visual context solely to enlarge the text.\nL276:   * Resizing: Take into account that your image might be resized if it is too large (see 【87†Resolution and token cost】); this might, for example, make text less legible. Consider pre-resizing your images, cropping them, or both. To have an oversized image rejected with an error instead of resized (important for 【67†coordinate workflows】), mark the image block with 【91†`\"oversized_image\": \"error\"`】.\nL277:   * Image compression: Compressing images before sending them, using a lossy format such as JPEG or WebP (lossy mode), can reduce latency by reducing the size of requests. However, this can introduce artifacts that are detrimental to model performance, especially when multiple compression passes are applied. For example, heavy JPEG compression can make text difficult to read. Confirm your compression settings are appropriate for the task by inspecting the actual images sent to the API.\nL278: * * *\nL279: ## Coordinates and bounding boxes\nL280: \nL281: For bounding boxes, points, and pixel coordinates, see 【67†Coordinates and bounding boxes】. Claude returns absolute pixel coordinates relative to the image it processes after resizing; that guide covers how Claude resizes and pads images and how to pre-resize or rescale so coordinates line up with your original image.\nL282: \nL283: * * *\nL284: ## Limitations\nL285: \nL286: Although Claude's image understanding capabilities are advanced, there are some limitations to be aware of:\nL287:   * People identification: Claude cannot be used to name people in images and refuses to do so (see the 【93†Usage Policy†www.anthropic.com】).\nL288:   * Accuracy: Claude might hallucinate or make mistakes when interpreting low-quality, rotated, or very small images under 200 pixels.\nL289:   * Spatial reasoning: Claude's coordinate and localization outputs are approximate. Follow the guidance in 【67†Coordinates and bounding boxes】 and verify outputs before relying on them.\nL290:   * Counting: Claude can give approximate counts of objects in an image but might not always be precisely accurate, especially with large numbers of small objects.\nL291:   * AI-generated images: Claude cannot determine whether an image is AI-generated and might be incorrect if asked. Do not rely on it to detect fake or synthetic images.\nL292:   * Inappropriate content: Claude does not process inappropriate or explicit images that violate the 【93†Usage Policy†www.anthropic.com】.\nL293:   * Healthcare applications: Although Claude can analyze general medical images, it is not designed to interpret complex diagnostic scans such as CTs or MRIs. Claude's outputs should not be considered a substitute for professional medical advice or diagnosis.\nL294: Always carefully review and verify Claude's image interpretations, especially for high-stakes use cases. Do not use Claude for tasks requiring perfect precision or sensitive image analysis without human oversight.\nL295: \nL296: * * *\nL297: ## FAQ\nL298: \nL299: ### What image file types does Claude support?\nL300: \nL301: JPEG, PNG, GIF, and WebP. See 【94†Supported formats】.\nL302: \nL303: ### Can Claude read image URLs?\nL304: \nL305: Yes. Use the `url` source type instead of `base64` in the `image` content block. See the 【95†URL-based image example】.\nL306: \nL307: ### Is there a limit to the image file size I can upload?\nL308: \nL309: Yes. See 【96†Request limits】 for per-image and overall request size limits across the Claude API, Amazon Bedrock, Claude Models on Google Cloud (Google managed), and claude.ai.\nL310: ### How many images can I include in one request?\nL311: \nL312: Up to 600 for each API request (100 for models with a 200k-token context window) and 20 for each turn on claude.ai. See 【96†Request limits】 for details and the lower per-image dimension limit that applies above 20 images.\nL313: \nL314: ### Does Claude read image metadata?\nL315: \nL316: No, Claude does not parse or receive any metadata from images passed to it.\nL317: ### Can I delete images I've uploaded?\nL318: \nL319: No. Image uploads are ephemeral and not stored beyond the duration of the API request. Uploaded images are automatically deleted after they have been processed.\nL320: \nL321: ### Where can I find details on data privacy for image uploads?\nL322: \nL323: Refer to the Anthropic privacy policy page for information on how uploaded images and other data are handled. Anthropic does not use uploaded images to train models.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCoordinates and bounding boxes - Claude Platform Docs (https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/vision-coordinates)\nciteturn12view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/vision-coordinates\",\"lineno\":87}); Total lines: 316\nL34: 【50†Tool reference】【51†Manage tool context】【52†Tool combinations】【53†Tool use with prompt caching】【54†Programmatic tool calling】【55†Fine-grained tool streaming】\nL35: \nL36: Context management\nL37: \nL47: 【66†Overview】【67†Coordinates and bounding boxes】\nL48: \nL49: Skills\nL50: \nL51: 【68†Overview】【69†Quickstart】【70†Best practices】【71†Skills for enterprise】【72†Skills in the API】\nL52: \nL53: MCP\nL54: \nL55: 【73†Remote MCP servers】【74†MCP connector】\nL56: \nL57: 【75†MCP tunnels】\nL58: \nL59: Claude on cloud platforms\nL60: \nL61: 【76†Amazon Bedrock (Opus 4.7 and later)】【77†Amazon Bedrock (Opus 4.6 and earlier)】【78†Claude Platform on AWS】【79†Microsoft Foundry】\nL62: \nL63: 【80†Google Cloud】\nL64: \nL65: 【81†Console】\nL66: \nL67: 【1†Messages】Images and vision\nL68: # Coordinates and bounding boxes\nL69: \nL70: How Claude resizes images, and how to work with the pixel coordinates it returns for bounding boxes, points, and UI elements.\nL71: \nL72: Copy page\nL73: \nL74: \nL75: \nL76: Claude can locate and label regions of an image (for example, returning bounding boxes for tables, form fields, chart elements, or UI components). This guide covers how Claude resizes images before processing them and how to work with the pixel coordinates it returns, so that boxes and points line up with your original image.\nL77: You'll need this for OCR pipelines, form extraction, chart parsing, UI element location, and any task where you act on a specific region of an image. For sending images, supported formats, and per-model resolution limits, see 【66†Vision】.\nL78: \nL79: \nL80: Claude works best with absolute pixel coordinates. Ask for them explicitly in your prompt. For example: \"Return the bounding box of each table as `[x1, y1, x2, y2]` (top-left and bottom-right corners) in pixel coordinates.\" Claude does not work well when you ask for normalized coordinates, for example: \"Return bounding box coordinates between `0` and `1000`.\" Always ask for pixel coordinates and normalize in your own code if you need to.\nL81: To get coordinates as machine-readable JSON instead of prose, define a schema with 【20†structured outputs】, for example an object with an `[x1, y1, x2, y2]` array for each detected element.\nL82: Coordinates follow the standard image convention: the origin `(0, 0)` is the top-left corner of the image, with x increasing to the right and y increasing downward. The coordinates Claude returns are pixel positions in the image Claude processes: your image after Claude resizes it to fit the model's native resolution (see 【82†How Claude resizes and pads images】).\nL83: To get coordinates you can use directly, either pre-resize your image so the coordinates map one-to-one onto the image you have (see 【83†Resize your image before uploading】), or rescale the coordinates Claude returns (see 【84†Rescale coordinates when you cannot pre-resize】).\nL84: \nL85: Claude's spatial reasoning has limits (see 【85†Limitations】). Coordinate accuracy is best when you state the expected coordinate format in your prompt and spot-check results visually before processing at scale. Small elements lose precision when an image is downscaled: for fine targets, crop the region of interest and send the crop (offset returned coordinates by the crop origin), or use a high-resolution-tier model.\nL86: For 【65†PDF support】, pages are rasterized to images server-side at dimensions you don't control, so the returned coordinates can't be reliably mapped back onto the page. To work with coordinates on PDF content, rasterize the pages to images yourself and use the pre-resize approach.\nL87: ## How Claude resizes and pads images\nL88: \nL89: Claude finds the largest aspect-preserving size that satisfies both of the model's image limits:\nL90: \nL91:   1. Edge limit: neither side exceeds the maximum edge length (1,568 px on the standard tier, 2,576 px on the high-resolution tier).\nL92:   2. Visual token limit: the image's token cost `⌈width / 28⌉ × ⌈height / 28⌉` does not exceed the model's visual token budget (1,568 tokens on the standard tier, 4,784 on the high-resolution tier).\nL93: See 【86†Resolution and token cost】 for which models are in which tier.\nL94: \nL95: For nearly all photos and screenshots, the visual token limit is what determines the final size. The edge limit takes over only for elongated images such as panoramas or tall phone screenshots. Compute the size with the 【83†reference implementation】 rather than scaling to the edge length by hand: a 1920×1080 screenshot resizes to 1456×819, not 1568×882, and assuming the edge limit puts every coordinate noticeably off target.\nL96: The token limit can also trigger a resize when neither side exceeds the edge limit. Overlooking this is the most common cause of misaligned coordinates. For example, an A4 page scanned at 130 DPI is 1075×1520 pixels: both sides are under 1,568 px, but it costs `39 × 55 = 2145` visual tokens, so Claude resizes it to 924×1307.\nL97: Claude then pads every image, resized or not, up to the next multiple of 28 pixels on the bottom and right edges (924×1307 becomes 924×1316 in the example). The padding contains no content: Claude perceives the padded image, but the page content only ever occupies the un-padded resized region. Always normalize or rescale by the resized dimensions, not the padded dimensions; dividing by the padded dimensions scales every coordinate by a small amount.\nL98: ## Resize your image before uploading\nL99: \nL100: The most reliable approach is to resize your image yourself before uploading, so the image you have is exactly the image Claude processes and the coordinates Claude returns need no conversion.\nL101: \nL102: First check which resolution tier your model is on (see 【86†Resolution and token cost】) and pass the matching edge and token limits. The following reference implementation computes the exact size Claude resizes an image to:\nL103: [Button: cURL][Button: CLI][Button: Python][Button: TypeScript][Button: C#][Button: Go][Button: Java][Button: PHP][Button: Ruby]\nL104: \nL105: \nL106:     `import math\nL107: \nL108: \nL109:     def count_image_tokens(width: int, height: int) -> int:\nL110:         \"\"\"Visual tokens consumed by an image: one token for each 28x28 pixel patch.\"\"\"\nL111:         return math.ceil(width / 28) * math.ceil(height / 28)\nL112: \nL113: \nL114:     def resized_size(\nL115:         width: int,\nL116:         height: int,\nL117:         max_edge: int = 1568,\nL118:         max_tokens: int = 1568,\nL119:     ) -> tuple[int, int]:\nL120:         \"\"\"The size Claude resizes an image to before padding.\nL121:         Defaults are for the standard resolution tier. For high-resolution-tier\nL122:         models, use max_edge=2576 and max_tokens=4784. Returns (width, height).\nL123:         Images that already fit within the limits are returned unchanged.\nL124:         \"\"\"\nL125: \nL126:         def fits(w: int, h: int) -> bool:\nL127:             return (\nL128:                 math.ceil(w / 28) * 28 <= max_edge\nL129:                 and math.ceil(h / 28) * 28 <= max_edge\nL130:                 and count_image_tokens(w, h) <= max_tokens\nL131:             )\nL132:         if fits(width, height):\nL133:             return (width, height)\nL134:         if height > width:\nL135:             resized_h, resized_w = resized_size(height, width, max_edge, max_tokens)\nL136:             return (resized_w, resized_h)\nL137: \nL138:         # Binary search along the long edge for the largest aspect-preserving\nL139:         # size that fits.\nL140:         aspect_ratio = width / height\nL141:         lo, hi = 1, width  # lo always fits; hi never fits\nL142:         while lo + 1 < hi:\nL143:             mid = (lo + hi) // 2\nL144:             if fits(mid, max(round(mid / aspect_ratio), 1)):\nL145:                 lo = mid\nL146:             else:\nL147:                 hi = mid\nL148:         return (lo, max(round(lo / aspect_ratio), 1))\nL149: \nL150: \nL151:     # The A4 example from \"How Claude resizes and pads images\":\nL152:     print(resized_size(1075, 1520))  # (924, 1307)\nL153: \nL154:     # To apply the resize, use your image library, for example Pillow:\nL155:     # image.resize(resized_size(*image.size))`\nL156:   1. Resize the image to the dimensions returned by the resize helper. If the image already fits within the model's limits, the helper returns its dimensions unchanged and no resize is needed.\nL157:   2. 【87†Send the resized image】 to the API. Don't pad it yourself. Claude handles padding, and padding doesn't shift the coordinate origin.\nL158:   3. In your prompt, ask explicitly for pixel coordinates. For example: \"Return the click point for the Submit button as `[x, y]` in pixel coordinates.\"\nL159:   4. Use the returned coordinates directly against the image you sent. If you need normalized coordinates, divide by the dimensions of the image you sent, not by the original image's dimensions and not by the padded dimensions.\nL160: ## Turn resizing into an error with `transformations`\nL161: Pre-resizing only protects your coordinates while your pipeline keeps producing the right sizes. A new image source or a switch to a model on a different resolution tier can quietly reintroduce server-side resizing. To turn that silent drift into a visible error, set the optional `transformations` field on an image content block in a 【88†Messages】 request:\nL162: \nL163:     `{\nL164:       \"type\": \"image\",\nL165:       \"source\": { \"type\": \"base64\", \"media_type\": \"image/png\", \"data\": \"...\" },\nL166:       \"transformations\": { \"oversized_image\": \"error\" }\nL167:     }`\nL168: \nL169: A request whose marked image (any block that sets `\"oversized_image\": \"error\"`) would be resized is rejected with a 400 `invalid_request_error` naming the image's dimensions and the largest dimensions that fit. Whether an image triggers the rejection depends on the 【82†limits of every model the request names】: the following 1920×1080 example is rejected by a standard-tier model but fits within the high-resolution tier:\nL170:     `messages.0.content.0: image dimensions 1920x1080 exceed the maximum image size of a model named on this request and would be downsized to 1456x819; scale the image to at most 1456x819 or set the image's oversized_image setting to \"downsize\"`\nL171: \nL172: \nL173: Rescale to the reported target and resend: the target is the largest size, at your image's aspect ratio, that every model named on the request accepts. How marked images interact with the 【89†server-side fallback beta】 is described with that feature; in every mode, a marked image is never served resized.\nL174: Each image has its own setting. `\"oversized_image\": \"downsize\"` (the default when the field is omitted) keeps automatic resizing as described on this page. Each image block is checked only against its own setting, so one request can mix images whose dimensions matter (a screenshot you'll click) with images where resizing is harmless (a logo). What the setting does and does not change:\nL175:   * 【82†Padding】 (which never discards content), format conversion, and orientation correction proceed as usual.\nL176:   * The hard limits (8,000 px on the longest side, and the stricter per-image limit on 【90†many-image requests】) are separate rejections; this setting never lets an image past them.\nL177:   * Images supplied by URL or file ID are checked once their bytes have been fetched; those rejections carry the same message without the leading position, so they don't identify which image failed; only embedded base64 images are named by position in the error.\nL178:   * 【65†PDF pages】 are rasterized server-side at dimensions you don't control; the `document` block does not accept the field (an image block nested inside a document's content accepts it like any other).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenRouter Image Inputs - Complete Documentation (https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/overview/multimodal/image-understanding)\nciteturn12view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/overview/multimodal/image-understanding\",\"lineno\":152}); Total lines: 187\nL0: > ## Documentation Index\nL1: >\nL2: > Fetch the complete documentation index at: 【0†/docs/llms.txt】\nL3: >\nL4: > Use this file to discover all available pages before exploring further.\nL5: \nL6: 【1†Skip to main content】\nL7: \nL8: 【2†OpenRouter | Documentation home page】\nL9: \nL10: Search...\nL11: \nL12: Navigation\nL13: \nL14: Multimodal\nL15: \nL16: Image Inputs\nL17: ### Overview\nL18: \nL19:   * 【3†Quickstart】\nL20:   * 【4†Principles】\nL21:   * 【5†Models】\nL22:   * Multimodal\nL23: \nL24:     * 【6†Overview】\nL25:     * 【